@page "/docs/cli"
<PageTitle>CLI - Blaizio</PageTitle>

<div class="typeset typeset-docs space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-2.5">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
            <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">CLI</h1>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">dotnet tool</BzBadge>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Outline">source you own</BzBadge>
        </div>
        <p>The styled layer is <em>your source, not a package</em>, and the Blaizio CLI (<code>blaizio</code>) is what puts it in your project. It copies components from a registry, rewrites them to your namespace, resolves their registry and NuGet dependencies, records what is installed and wires the Tailwind toolchain. A component you add today is a file you can open, read and edit tomorrow.</p>
    </header>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Install &amp; update the tool</h2>
        <p>The CLI ships as a global <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/core/tools/global-tools">dotnet tool</a>, so <code>dotnet tool</code> installs and updates it. <code>blaizio update</code> does something different: it moves your <em>project's</em> packages and components forward and never touches the tool itself (see its section below).</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Install" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Global options</h2>
        <p>Every command accepts these options. <code>--yes</code>, <code>--json</code> or <code>--silent</code> makes a run non-interactive, so there are no prompts and the defaults win. With <code>--json</code>, results go to stdout as machine-readable JSON and errors go to stderr, which suits scripts, IDE plugins and MCP clients.</p>
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Option</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Meaning</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-c, --cwd &lt;DIR&gt;</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Working directory (defaults to the current directory).</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-y, --yes</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Skip prompts, accept defaults.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-s, --silent</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Mute output.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--json</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Emit JSON.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--registry &lt;URL&gt;</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Registry base URL or local path. Overrides the value in <code>blaizio.json</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
        <p>Exit codes: <code>0</code> success · <code>1</code> error, including drift found by <code>add --diff</code> · <code>2</code> registry error · <code>130</code> canceled (Ctrl+C, or Esc at any prompt). <code>blaizio help [command]</code>, or <code>-h</code> on any command, prints usage. Set <code>BLAIZIO_DEBUG=1</code> for full stack traces.</p>
        <p>Command aliases: <code>create</code> = <code>new</code> · <code>rm</code> = <code>remove</code> · <code>un</code> = <code>uninstall</code> · <code>list</code> = <code>search</code>.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">From a solution root</h2>
        <p>Every command that works on a project also works from a folder above it, such as a solution root. When you run it in a folder with no <code>blaizio.json</code> and no <code>.csproj</code>, it looks for Blaizio projects underneath, skipping build output, <code>node_modules</code> and similar folders.</p>
        <ul>
            <li><b>One project found:</b> the command runs there, and a <code>project</code> line tells you which one.</li>
            <li><b>Several found:</b> you get a list with every project checked. <kbd>Space</kbd> toggles one, <kbd>A</kbd> toggles all, <kbd>Esc</kbd> cancels, and <kbd>Enter</kbd> runs the command in each selected project in turn.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Each run prints under its own header, and the summary shows a tick or cross per project along with the worst exit code. One project failing does not stop the others.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/SolutionRoot" />
        <p>Each project keeps its own <code>blaizio.json</code>, registry, style and record of installed files. There is no solution-level configuration: a run over several projects is exactly the command run in each folder in turn. A few rules apply:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><code>--yes</code>, or having no terminal to ask (as in CI), selects every project.</li>
            <li><code>--json</code> refuses to run against several projects. It names them and asks for <code>-c</code>, because two JSON documents on one stdout are not valid JSON.</li>
            <li><code>search</code>, <code>view</code> and <code>docs</code> answer a question rather than change a project, so they ask you to pick one project instead of repeating the same answer.</li>
            <li>Only <code>update</code> also lists projects that reference the Blaizio packages without a <code>blaizio.json</code>, and it runs only its package bump there (<a href="#update">details</a>).</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio new</h2>
        <p>Use <code>new</code> to start an app from <b>nothing</b>. It scaffolds the project from a template, runs the whole wiring pipeline over it (config, packages, tokens file, host page, Tailwind) and adds the template's component set, so one command gives you a running app. <code>create</code> works as an alias.</p>
        <p>Each verb means one thing: <code>new</code> starts an app, <code>add</code> wires Blaizio into an app you already have and copies components, <code>apply</code> changes the theme, <code>remove</code> takes a component back out, and <code>uninstall</code> removes Blaizio from the project.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/New" />
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Template</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>What you get</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>showcase</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>A complete WebAssembly demo app: layout with sidebar, command palette and dark mode toggle; dashboard, forms, overlays, data and auth pages; ~35 components pre-added. The best way to see everything working.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>library</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>A Razor class library wired for Blaizio (Razor SDK, framework reference, packages, imports), for building a shared component library. An existing bare class library gets the same wiring in place.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>webapp</code> / <code>wasm</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Config, packages and styling are wired, but the page scaffolding for these templates is still in progress. For now, run <code>dotnet new</code> first and then <code>blaizio add</code> inside the new project.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">a registry reference</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Any <code>registry:template</code> item, given as a name, <code>@@namespace/name</code> or URL. It scaffolds the template's files (tokens substituted), wires the project and installs its component set. A direct URL shows a warning first, because a template is source code that becomes your whole app.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Option</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Default</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Meaning</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>[TEMPLATE]</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>prompted</BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>showcase</code>, <code>webapp</code>, <code>wasm</code> or <code>library</code> (see the table above).</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-n, --name</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>directory name</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Project name for the scaffolded app.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-d, --defaults</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>off</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>No prompts, and the template defaults to <code>showcase</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-f, --force</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>off</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Overwrite existing scaffold files and <code>blaizio.json</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--style</code> · <code>-p, --preset</code> · <code>--icons</code> · <code>-ns, --namespace</code> · <code>-o, --output</code> · <code>--tailwind</code> · <code>--rtl</code> · <code>--pointer</code> · <code>--scrollbar</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>-</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Styling and wiring options, passed through to the wiring pipeline with the same meaning and defaults as the <code>add</code> options below.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio add</h2>
        <p>Use <code>add</code> to copy components into an <b>existing app</b>. When the project has no <code>blaizio.json</code> yet, the same command wires Blaizio in first. With no arguments it opens an interactive picker (<BzKbd>Space</BzKbd> toggles one, <BzKbd>A</BzKbd> toggles all, <BzKbd>Enter</BzKbd> confirms). The wiring step edits your existing project and never creates new pages:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>writes <code>blaizio.json</code> and installs the base NuGet packages (<code>Blaizio.Base</code>, <code>Blaizio.Icons</code>, <code>Blaizio.Icons.Tabler</code>, <code>TailwindMerge.NET</code>);</li>
            <li>creates the <b>tokens file</b> (<code>Styles/app.css</code>), one file you own that holds the Tailwind input, your <code>:root</code>/<code>.dark</code> theme values with the chosen preset merged in, and the token → utility map;</li>
            <li>git-ignores <code>.blaizio/</code>, where the <code>Blaizio.Base</code> package writes the static contract sheets on every <code>dotnet build</code> (generated output, like <code>obj/</code>);</li>
            <li>wires a Tailwind pipeline (see <code>blaizio tailwind</code> below);</li>
            <li>patches the <b>host page</b> the app already has, inserting the compiled stylesheet link and the pre-paint <code>boot.js</code> script: <code>wwwroot/index.html</code> (WASM), <code>Components/App.razor</code> (Blazor Web App) or <code>Pages/_Host.cshtml</code> / <code>_Layout.cshtml</code> (Blazor Server).</li>
        </ul>
        <p>On a project that is already initialized, the wiring step runs again as a <b>top-up</b> whenever you pass a wiring flag (<code>--style</code>, <code>--tailwind</code>, <code>--rtl</code>, <code>--pointer</code>, <code>--css</code>, <code>-f</code>). The recorded config is kept, and explicit flags override it. The one exception is <code>--style</code> once components are installed: they keep the recorded style, and only a full <code>apply</code> changes it (see <code>--style</code> below). A wiring-only run such as <code>blaizio add --rtl</code> is a complete operation on its own.</p>
        <p>Components carry their style <em>inlined in their classes</em>, so there is no per-style stylesheet and no <code>style-*</code> class on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>. Page direction is never touched either: <code>--rtl</code> prepares the components, and setting <code>dir="rtl"</code> stays your decision.</p>
        <p>Then, for each component, the CLI:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>resolves its <em>registry dependencies</em> transitively (dependency-first order, each item once) and installs the aggregated <em>NuGet dependencies</em>;</li>
            <li>writes the files under your output directory and rewrites every namespace to yours;</li>
            <li>tops up <code>_Imports.razor</code>, plus a project-wide global using for the base namespace;</li>
            <li>records the item in <code>blaizio.json</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Existing files are <b>skipped</b> unless you pass <code>--overwrite</code>, so your edits are safe by default.</p>
        <p>Even <code>--overwrite</code> will not silently discard your work. The CLI records a content hash in <code>blaizio.json</code> for every file it writes, so a later run can tell <b>a file you changed</b> apart from <b>a file that has a newer version upstream</b>. Untouched files are replaced without a prompt. The ones you edited appear in a checkbox picker (<kbd>Space</kbd> to toggle, <kbd>A</kbd> for all, <kbd>Enter</kbd> to confirm, <kbd>Esc</kbd> to cancel), and only the ones you tick are replaced. <a href="#local-edits">Your local edits</a> explains what happens when nobody is there to answer.</p>
        <p>The registry is <b>checked before any work starts</b>. Wiring runs before components are fetched, so without that check an unreachable registry would install packages, write your tokens file and edit the host page before failing. A single request up front turns that into a clean refusal (exit 2) that leaves the project untouched. <code>update</code> makes the same check before bumping packages. A registry that answers but ships no <code>index.json</code> is fine, because items still resolve at the base path.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Add" />
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Option</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Meaning</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>[COMPONENTS]</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Names, URLs, or local <code>.json</code> paths. Leave it empty for the interactive picker. <code>name@1.2.0</code> pins the item to a version the registry stamps: the pin is recorded, <code>update</code>/<code>--diff</code> re-request exactly it, dependencies landing on a pinned name respect it, and a later plain <code>add name</code> unpins.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-a, --all</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Add every component in the registry.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--overwrite</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Overwrite existing files (default: skip them). Components you changed since install are asked about first, never replaced silently.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--prune</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Delete files in the output directory no registry item ships anymore (requires <code>--all</code>).</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--dry-run</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Resolve and report only. Nothing is written or installed.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--no-deps</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Skip NuGet installs <em>and</em> transitive registry dependencies.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--no-nuget</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Skip only the NuGet install (monorepo / ProjectReference setups).</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-o, --output</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Destination override for this run.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-ns, --namespace</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Namespace override for this run.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-p, --preset</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Apply a color preset as part of the add, saving a second <code>apply</code> command. Pass a name (<code>nova</code>, the default, <code>aurora</code>, <code>comet</code>, <code>corona</code>, <code>eclipse</code>, <code>equinox</code>, <code>magnetar</code>, <code>meteor</code>, <code>nebula</code>, <code>polaris</code>, <code>pulsar</code>, <code>quasar</code>, <code>solstice</code>, <code>umbra</code>, <code>vesper</code>, <code>zenith</code>) or a <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="themes">Themes</a> code. A code can also carry a chart palette, radius scale, heading/body fonts and an icon set. Its values are baked into the tokens file, and its icon set is applied exactly as <code>--icons</code> would. A webfont selection also links the Google Fonts stylesheet in the host page. Takes precedence over <code>blaizio.json</code>. On an initialized project this is a full apply: your components re-install, with the flag as the consent. A <code>--style</code> next to it overrides the code's style.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--style</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Component style for the wiring step: <code>ash</code>, <code>aura</code>, <code>ember</code> (default), <code>flint</code>, <code>forge</code>, <code>glow</code>, <code>spark</code>, <code>wisp</code>. On an initialized project with nothing installed it re-records the style. Once components are installed they keep the recorded style. The CLI says so and prints the full <code>apply</code> (with a ready code) that switches style by re-installing them. <code>--json</code> names the skipped style as <code>styleNotApplied</code>; the deprecated <code>skinNotApplied</code> key carries the same value. With <code>-p</code> the style goes through the preset's apply instead, and the switch happens in that one run.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--icons</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>The icon set the components draw from: <code>tabler</code> (default), <code>lucide</code>, <code>phosphor</code>, <code>remix</code>, <code>hugeicons</code>. Installs that set's package <em>instead of</em> Tabler's and lands <code>BzGlyphs.cs</code> pointed at it. On an initialized project it runs <code>apply --icons</code> (below). See <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="docs/cli#icon-sets">Icon sets</a>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--tailwind</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Tailwind pipeline for the wiring step: <code>auto</code>, <code>standalone</code>, <code>node</code>, <code>vite</code>, <code>rollup</code>, <code>postcss</code>, <code>none</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--rtl</code> / <code>--pointer</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Enable RTL support / the pointer cursor on buttons (wiring step). RTL also installs <code>direction-provider</code> once; components read the reading direction from it.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--scrollbar</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Thin themed scrollbars (wiring step): appends the <code>scrollbar-thin</code> block to your tokens file, which activates the <code>scrollbar-thin</code> classes the components already use. See <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="docs/utilities/scrollbar">Scrollbar</a>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-d, --defaults</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>No prompts in the wiring step, all defaults.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--force-overwrite</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>With <code>--overwrite</code>, also replace the components you changed, without asking. It is separate from <code>-f</code> on purpose: discarding your file edits should not also reset the project's wiring.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-y, --yes</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>No prompts. With <code>--overwrite</code> that means <b>your changed components are kept</b>, because an unattended run never discards edits nobody was asked about.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-f, --force</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Re-wire from scratch (overwrite <code>blaizio.json</code>) before adding. It has nothing to do with component files, which is what <code>--force-overwrite</code> is for.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--diff [path]</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Compare installed files against the registry instead of writing (the optional value filters to one file path). Exits <code>1</code> on any drift, so it works as a CI check, like <code>git diff --exit-code</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--view [path]</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Print file contents instead of writing (the optional value filters to one file path).</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--css &lt;path&gt;</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Record a custom Tailwind input for bundler setups. The Blaizio imports are then kept in sync inside <em>this</em> file instead of the CLI-managed <code>Styles/app.css</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
    </section>

    <section id="update">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio update</h2>
        <p><code>update</code> moves the whole stack forward <b>in lockstep</b>. First it bumps the Blaizio NuGet packages to this tool's pinned versions: <code>Blaizio.Base</code>, <code>Blaizio.Icons</code>, the package of the icon set the project records (<code>Blaizio.Icons.Tabler</code> unless <code>--icons</code> picked another), any other <code>Blaizio.Icons.*</code> the csproj references, and <code>TailwindMerge.NET</code>. Then it re-pulls components from your style's registry variants, overwriting local copies: everything recorded in <code>blaizio.json</code>, or only the ones you name. It is one command on purpose. Components rely on the package's JS/CSS assets, so syncing one half without the other leaves them out of step (for example, a component importing a <code>dist/</code> module that the older package does not carry).</p>
        <p>Your tokens file is never touched: it is yours. The contract sheets in <code>.blaizio/</code> come from the <code>Blaizio.Base</code> package, so they always match its version. A project still on the old <code>Styles/blaizio/</code> layout (CLI v1) is migrated to the current layout first, after you confirm: components are re-installed from the style's inlined variants, the tokens file is composed from your existing values, and <code>Styles/blaizio/</code> is deleted. <code>update</code> does not update the tool itself; use <code>dotnet tool update -g Blaizio.Cli</code> for that.</p>
        <p>A project that only references the Blaizio packages, such as a class library whose <code>.csproj</code> has <code>Blaizio.Base</code> but no <code>blaizio.json</code> beside it, gets <b>only the package bump</b>. The ids its <code>.csproj</code> already references are pinned to the tool's versions, and nothing is introduced or recorded. A solution-root run finds such projects next to the wired ones, so no sibling is left behind on an older <code>Blaizio.Base</code>.</p>
        <p><b>Each entry is re-pulled from the registry it was installed from.</b> A plain name means this project's <code>registry</code>; a name recorded as <code>@("@ns/name")</code> goes to that namespace's registry instead. So a component that arrived from somewhere else has to be <a href="docs/registry/namespaces">recorded with its namespace</a>. Otherwise <code>update</code> looks for it in the default registry and stops with <em>"is recorded in blaizio.json, but this project's registry does not serve it"</em>. To fix that, record the source and reinstall the component once (<CmdChip Command="blaizio registry add" /> then <code>blaizio add @("@ns/name")</code>), or drop it with <CmdChip Command="blaizio remove" /> if it is gone for good.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Update" />
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Option</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Meaning</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>[COMPONENTS]</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Components to re-pull. Empty re-pulls everything recorded in <code>blaizio.json</code>; the package bump runs either way.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-y, --yes</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>No prompts, so <b>every component you changed is kept</b> and the rest update. The safe shape for CI and scripts.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-f, --force</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Take the upstream version of everything, including the components you changed. The only flag that discards local edits.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--dry-run</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Report what would change, without bumping packages or writing files.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
    </section>

    <section id="local-edits">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Your local edits</h2>
        <p>Components are <b>copied into your project</b>, so editing them is the point. <code>update</code> and <code>add --overwrite</code> re-pull those same files, so the CLI keeps a baseline to tell a file you changed apart from a file with a newer version upstream. Every write records the content hash of what it wrote next to that file's path in <code>blaizio.json</code> (<code>installed[].files[].hash</code>). Nothing else is stored, nothing leaves your machine, and line endings are normalized so a CRLF checkout is not mistaken for an edit.</p>
        <p>Each file ends up in one of four cases, and only the last one interrupts you:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>identical to upstream: left alone and reported as <em>already up to date</em>;</li>
            <li>untouched since install, with a new version upstream: replaced;</li>
            <li>changed by you, with no new version upstream: left alone;</li>
            <li>changed by you <em>and</em> updated upstream: you decide.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>How you decide depends on how the command runs:</p>
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Run</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>What happens to the files you changed</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Interactive</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>A checkbox list of exactly those files, each labeled with its component. Tick the ones to replace (<code>a</code> toggles all), leave the rest. Unticked keeps your version. The pick is per file, so a component can keep the file you extended and still take upstream for the one you only patched.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>-y</code>, <code>--json</code>, <code>--silent</code>, no TTY</BzTableCell><BzTableCell><b>Kept.</b> <code>-y</code> means "don't ask me", not "overwrite everything", because a scheduled update must never destroy work nobody was asked about. Everything else still updates, and the kept files are listed at the end under their component (<code>--json</code> carries them as <code>updated.decisions</code>, one entry per file with <code>kept</code> true or false).</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>update --force</code><br /><code>add --overwrite --force-overwrite</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><b>Replaced.</b> The explicit "throw my changes away" switch, and the only way to lose them unattended. There are two names because <code>add -f</code> already means "re-write <code>blaizio.json</code>".</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
        <p>The same record answers a second question: <b>what upstream stopped shipping</b>. When a component renames or splits a file, the old copy would otherwise stay in your project, still compiling and still resolving its type, and later surface as a puzzling "cannot convert" error at some call site. An overwriting run removes those files. It only touches paths recorded in <code>blaizio.json</code>, never sweeps your output directory, and never removes a file that another installed component still ships. A file that still matches its baseline is provably untouched, so it goes. One you changed, or one with no baseline recorded, is listed as left on disk, and only <code>--force</code> removes it. Either way it stays in the record, so <code>uninstall</code> can still take it back.</p>
        <p>A kept component stays flagged: its baseline is not moved forward, so the next <code>update</code> offers it again. To see what you would be giving up first, run <code>blaizio add --diff &lt;component&gt;</code>. Entries written before the CLI recorded hashes have no baseline, so those components are treated as changed (listed as <em>no baseline recorded</em>) until the first run that rewrites them.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/LocalEdits" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio apply</h2>
        <p>Use <code>apply</code> to restyle an existing project from a preset. It applies the color tokens, plus whatever a <a href="themes">Themes</a> code carries (style, chart palette, radius, heading/body fonts, icon set). It has two modes, plus one flag that works on its own:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Scoped</b> (<code>--only theme</code> / <code>fonts</code> / <code>tokens</code> / <code>icons</code>): targeted value patches in the tokens file that leave your other edits in place. <code>icons</code> is the one scoped part that touches the csproj and a component file, because it retargets <code>BzGlyphs.cs</code> and installs the set's package. A scoped run keeps the style recorded in <code>blaizio.json</code>. When the code names another style, it says so and points you to the full apply, the only mode that switches style.</li>
            <li><b><code>--icons &lt;set&gt;</code> alone</b>: no preset at all, only the icon set swap. This is the most direct form; see <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="docs/cli#icon-sets">Icon sets</a>.</li>
            <li><b>Full</b>: the style swap, destructive by design. The look lives inlined in the component files, so every recorded component is re-installed from the target style's registry variants, overwriting local edits, and then the tokens are patched. Commit or stash beforehand; the CLI confirms before it writes. This is the one place edits are <em>not</em> offered back to you, because keeping a component would leave it in the old style next to everything else. The confirmation covers all of them, and <code>-y</code> accepts it.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>A webfont selection patches <code>--font-heading</code> / the document <code>font-family</code> into the tokens file and adds the Google Fonts stylesheet to the host page as a <code>&lt;link&gt;</code> marked <code>data-blaizio="fonts"</code>, so a later apply can swap it and <code>uninstall</code> removes it. <b>Your fonts win by default</b>: when the project already defines its own typography (an <code>@@font-face</code>, a hand-written <code>--font-heading</code>, a document <code>font-family</code>, or an unmarked webfont link), a full apply skips the preset's fonts and says so. Pass <code>--only fonts</code> to override that explicitly.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Apply" />
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Option</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Meaning</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>[PRESET]</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>A preset name (<code>comet</code>, <code>eclipse</code>, ...) or a <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="themes">Themes</a> preset code (e.g. <code>32r</code>). Prompted when omitted, unless <code>--icons</code> is given, since that needs no preset.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--icons &lt;set&gt;</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Point the components at an icon set: <code>tabler</code>, <code>lucide</code>, <code>phosphor</code>, <code>remix</code>, <code>hugeicons</code>. Alone it is the whole apply; with a preset or code it overrides the code's own icon segment. <code>--icons tabler</code> is the way back to the default.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--only &lt;parts&gt;</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Restrict to parts of the preset: <code>theme</code>, <code>fonts</code>, <code>tokens</code>, <code>icons</code> (comma-separated). <code>--only fonts</code> also overrides a detected user font setup; <code>--only icons</code> applies the set the code carries (same as <code>--icons</code> with that set) and nothing else.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--pointer</code> / <code>--scrollbar</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Also wire the pointer cursor on buttons / the thin themed scrollbars into the tokens file. These are the same toggles <code>new</code> and <code>add</code> take, so applying a Themes selection to an existing app is one command.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc id="icon-sets" class="scroll-mt-20">Icon sets</h2>
        <p>The styled components never name an icon set. The 42 glyphs they draw with (the check in a select item, the chevron on a trigger, the grip on a sortable row, the spinner) resolve through one file that the <code>utils</code> item puts in your components folder: <code>BzGlyphs.cs</code>. Which set that file points at is a project setting with a default and one flag:</p>
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>You want</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Run</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>What happens</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">The default</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>nothing (omit <code>--icons</code>)</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Tabler. <code>Blaizio.Icons.Tabler</code> is installed with the base packages and <code>BzGlyphs.cs</code> ships pointed at <code>Tabler.Outline.*</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Another set, new app</BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>blaizio new showcase --icons lucide</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>The scaffolded csproj references <code>Blaizio.Icons.Lucide</code> instead of Tabler's package; every copied component draws from Lucide from the first build.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Another set, existing app, first install</BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>blaizio add button --icons lucide</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Same as above through the wiring step: Lucide's package in place of Tabler's, <code>BzGlyphs.cs</code> lands retargeted, <code>blaizio.json</code> records <code>"icons": "lucide"</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Switch a project already wired</BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>blaizio apply --icons phosphor</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Rewrites the members of <code>BzGlyphs.cs</code> to the new set, installs its package, records the choice. Nothing else in the project moves; <code>add --icons</code> on an initialized project does exactly this.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Back to the default</BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>blaizio apply --icons tabler</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Same rewrite towards Tabler; the record clears.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">A set from a Themes code</BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>blaizio apply 32.1</code> / <code>add -p 32.1</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>The Icons knob on <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="themes">Themes</a> adds a <code>.N</code> segment to the code; the code applies it the way <code>--icons</code> would. An explicit <code>--icons</code> beside a code wins.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
        </BzTable>
        <p>Three things to know:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><b>The CLI never removes a package.</b> After a switch, the previous set's package stays referenced and the summary says so. Remove it yourself if your own code does not use that set directly.</li>
            <li><b><code>blaizio update</code> follows the record.</b> Re-pulling <code>utils</code> lands <code>BzGlyphs.cs</code> pointed at the recorded set, with a recorded hash to match, so the file never reads as a local edit. The package bump also moves the recorded set's package rather than reintroducing Tabler's.</li>
            <li><b>Your own icons are untouched.</b> <code>--icons</code> only decides what the <em>components</em> draw with. <code>&lt;BzIcon Icon="@@Tabler.Outline.Home" /&gt;</code> in your pages keeps working as long as that set's package is referenced, and you can add any set by hand with <code>dotnet add package Blaizio.Icons.&lt;Set&gt;</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>You can also retarget a single glyph by hand in <code>BzGlyphs.cs</code>. The CLI leaves any member its table does not know exactly as you wrote it.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio contrast</h2>
        <p>Run <code>contrast</code> after you change a theme. It parses the <code>:root</code>/<code>.dark</code> values in the tokens file and measures the color pairs the components actually paint against <b>WCAG AA</b>, in both modes: every foreground-on-surface pair, the tokens used as text on the page background, the focus ring (3:1 for non-text) and the button's derived dark fill. Failures list the pair and its ratio and exit with code <code>1</code>, so it works as a CI check just like <code>add --diff</code>.</p>
        <p>The primary color plays two roles, link text on the page and a fill under the button label, so a single value cannot always clear AA for both. Those pairs report as warnings, never failures, and the default button derives an AA-safe dark fill regardless.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Contrast" />
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Option</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Meaning</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--all</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>List the passing pairs too, not just failures and warnings.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--css &lt;PATH&gt;</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Audit a custom tokens file instead of the recorded one.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>--json</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Emit the findings as JSON.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio docs</h2>
        <p>Prints a component's metadata, its docs page URL and the full parameter reference, parsed from the registry source. You get the API without scraping the website, which helps agents and IDE plugins as much as people.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Docs" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio view</h2>
        <p>Prints a component's metadata, dependencies and full file contents without writing anything. It is the read-only counterpart of <code>add</code>: the same item resolution, but nothing touches the project.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/View" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio search</h2>
        <p>Lists registry components from the configured registry, or from the <code>@@namespaces</code>, URLs or paths you pass as extra arguments. <code>-q</code> filters by name, title or description, <code>-t</code> by item type, and <code>--category</code> by the registry's category tags. <code>-l</code> sets the page size (default 100) and <code>-o, --offset</code> the start. (<code>list</code> remains as an alias.)</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Search" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio remove</h2>
        <p>Use <code>remove</code> to take individual components back out. Like <code>uninstall</code>, it works strictly <b>by record</b>: it deletes exactly the files <code>add</code> wrote for each named item, plus its <code>blaizio.json</code> entry. Files you authored under the output directory are never swept up, and a file that two components share survives while either is installed. Names resolve however you type them (<code>ColorPicker</code>, <code>colorpicker</code>, <code>color-picker</code>).</p>
        <p>It refuses to break the project. An item that another installed component still depends on is reported and skipped (exit 1) unless you pass <code>-f, --force</code>. Packages and wiring are left alone: components that are now unreferenced, and NuGet packages nothing needs anymore, are listed so you can decide what to do with them. It asks before touching anything (<code>-y</code> skips the prompt), and <code>--dry-run</code> previews the result. <span class="text-muted-foreground">(<code>rm</code> works as an alias.)</span></p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Remove" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio uninstall</h2>
        <p>Use <code>uninstall</code> to reverse <code>add</code>, wiring and components alike. It works strictly <b>by record</b> and removes:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>the tracked components and NuGet packages, exactly as <code>blaizio.json</code> recorded them at install time, along with their <code>@@using</code> registrations;</li>
            <li>the tokens file, if the wiring created it (a file you already had only loses the Blaizio import and source lines);</li>
            <li>the <code>.blaizio/</code> contract folder and its <code>.gitignore</code> entry, and the standalone Tailwind targets;</li>
            <li>the host page wiring (<code>boot.js</code>, the stylesheet link and the Google Fonts link) and <code>blaizio.json</code> itself.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Anything you authored, or referenced before the CLI ran, stays. It asks before touching anything (<code>-y</code> skips the prompt), and <code>--dry-run</code> previews the result. <span class="text-muted-foreground">(<code>un</code> remains as an alias.)</span></p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Uninstall" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio eject</h2>
        <p>Use <code>eject</code> when you want to <b>own</b> the generated styling layer instead of tracking it. It copies the contract sheets <em>into</em> your tokens file and removes the <code>.blaizio/</code> imports, so the styling plumbing is frozen at the current version and yours to edit from then on. No dependency is removed: <code>Blaizio.Base</code> stays for behavior and JS.</p>
        <p>Ejecting cannot be undone, since a later <code>Blaizio.Base</code> bump no longer reaches the ejected copy, so the command asks first (<code>-y</code> skips the prompt). It prefers the generated <code>.blaizio/</code> sheets, which match your installed package, and falls back to the CLI's embedded copies when the project was never built. If you also want the build to stop generating the now unused sheets, add <code>&lt;BlaizioMaterializeContract&gt;false&lt;/BlaizioMaterializeContract&gt;</code> to the csproj afterwards.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Eject" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio info</h2>
        <p>Shows the resolved project and its configuration: tool version, csproj, namespace, output directory, style, preset, registry and what's installed.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Info" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio tailwind</h2>
        <p>Blaizio needs Tailwind v4 to compile <code>Styles/app.css</code> into <code>wwwroot/app.css</code>, and the <code>tailwind</code> subcommands inspect and wire that pipeline. <code>auto</code> prefers an existing setup (<code>vite</code> &gt; <code>rollup</code> &gt; <code>postcss</code> &gt; <code>node</code> &gt; <code>standalone</code>) and never overwrites a bundler you already have. Even a bundler config that does not have its Tailwind plugin <em>yet</em> wins, in which case the CLI prints the manual step instead of wiring <code>standalone</code> over it. On a bare project, <code>auto</code> picks <code>standalone</code>.</p>
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Subcommand</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Purpose</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>tailwind detect</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Report which pipelines are present and which one <code>auto</code> would pick.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>tailwind setup --mode &lt;ID&gt;</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Wire a pipeline: <code>auto</code>, <code>standalone</code>, <code>node</code>, <code>vite</code>, <code>rollup</code>, <code>postcss</code>, <code>none</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>tailwind fetch</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Pre-fetch the standalone binary (CI / offline); sha256-verified.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Tailwind" />
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Pipeline</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>How it compiles</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>standalone</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><b>Zero Node.</b> Setup writes <code>.blaizio/Blaizio.Tailwind.targets</code> and imports it into your csproj, so the native <code>tailwindcss</code> binary runs on every <code>dotnet build</code> / <code>dotnet watch</code>. The binary auto-downloads into a per-user cache on first build (or pre-fetch it, sha256-verified, with <code>tailwind fetch</code>). MSBuild knobs: <code>BlaizioTailwindVersion</code>, <code>BlaizioTailwindAutoFetch</code>, <code>BlaizioTailwindInput/Output/Exe</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>node</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Adds <code>@@tailwindcss/cli</code> as a devDependency plus <code>css</code> / <code>css:watch</code> scripts to <code>package.json</code> (package manager detected from your lockfile: pnpm / yarn / bun / npm). You run the install yourself.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>vite</code> / <code>rollup</code> / <code>postcss</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Detected and respected when already present. The CLI reports them but does not set them up. Vite uses <code>@@tailwindcss/vite</code>; Rollup and PostCSS-based bundlers (webpack etc.) host <code>@@tailwindcss/postcss</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>none</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Writes only the input CSS; compiling is yours to arrange.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
        <p><code>tailwind fetch</code> options: <code>--tw-version</code> (a release tag or <code>latest</code>; default <code>v4.1.11</code>), <code>--musl</code> (Alpine builds; auto-detected), <code>-f, --force</code> (re-download). The cache lives under <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\blaizio\cache</code> (Windows), <code>~/Library/Caches/blaizio</code> (macOS) or <code>~/.cache/blaizio</code> (Linux). Set the <code>BLAIZIO_CACHE_DIR</code> environment variable to move it. One download serves every project.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio preset</h2>
        <p>The <code>preset</code> subcommands work with <a href="themes">Themes</a> preset codes: compact strings that pack a style, color preset, chart palette, radius, fonts, RTL and an icon set into a few characters (for example <code>32r</code>, or <code>32.1</code> with Lucide). <code>new -p</code>, <code>add -p</code> and <code>apply</code> all accept codes directly, and these subcommands are for inspecting and sharing them.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Preset" />
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Subcommand</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Purpose</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>preset decode &lt;CODE&gt;</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Show what a code carries: style, preset, chart, radius, fonts, RTL and icon set.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>preset resolve</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>The inverse: read the project's recorded selections and print the code that reproduces them.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>preset url &lt;CODE&gt;</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Print the Themes page URL for a code.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>preset open &lt;CODE&gt;</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Open that URL in the browser.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio.json</h2>
        <p><code>blaizio.json</code> holds the per-project configuration. The wiring step in <code>add</code> writes it, and every install keeps it current. Commit it to source control: the <code>installed</code> map is what powers <code>add --diff</code> and <code>update</code> with no arguments, and each file's <code>hash</code> is the baseline behind <a href="#local-edits">Your local edits</a>, recording what that file looked like when the CLI wrote it. A file recorded as a bare path string, or without a <code>hash</code>, has no baseline.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Config" />
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Key</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Default</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Meaning</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>namespace</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>required</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Root namespace applied to every copied component.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>output</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>Components/Ui</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Where copied components land (project-relative).</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>style</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>ember</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>The style the installed components wear, and whose inlined variants <code>add</code> (and <code>update</code>) install. A full <code>blaizio apply</code> changes it (components re-install); <code>add --style</code> changes it only while nothing is installed. Older files may carry it as <code>theme</code>, which is still read.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>preset</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>nova</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>The color preset whose values are merged into the tokens file.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>css</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>null</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Custom Tailwind input for bundler setups. When set, the CLI keeps the Blaizio imports in sync inside <em>this</em> file instead of owning <code>Styles/app.css</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>cssCreated</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>false</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Whether the wiring created the tokens file itself. <code>uninstall</code> deletes the file only in that case; a file you already had stays yours.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>ejected</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>false</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Set by <code>eject</code>: the contract lives inline in the tokens file, and no command re-manages the <code>.blaizio/</code> imports.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>rtl</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>false</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>RTL <i>support</i> was requested at wiring time (the components mirror through logical properties). It never sets <code>dir="rtl"</code>, because page direction stays the app's choice.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>icons</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>tabler</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>The icon set the components draw from (<code>--icons</code>); absent means Tabler. <code>update</code> re-pulls <code>BzGlyphs.cs</code> against it and moves that set's package.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>registry</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>https://blaiz.io/r</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Registry base URL, or a local path (resolved against the project). <code>--registry</code> overrides per run.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>aliases</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>base: Blaizio</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Namespace aliases. <code>base</code> is the headless layer's namespace (it drives the generated usings), and <code>ui</code> mirrors <code>namespace</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>installed</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>{}</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Every added item and the files it wrote, maintained by the CLI.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
        <h3 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Changing your choices later</h3>
        <p>Nothing you picked during the first <code>add</code> is locked in. <code>blaizio info</code> shows the current values, <code>preset resolve</code> prints them as a shareable <a href="themes">Themes</a> code, and each setting has a command that changes it:</p>
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Setting</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Change it with</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Color preset</BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>blaizio apply &lt;name|code&gt;</code> patches the tokens file in place.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Style</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>A <b>full</b> <code>blaizio apply</code> with a <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="themes">Themes</a> code that carries the new style. The look lives inlined in the component files, so every recorded component re-installs from the new style's variants (the CLI confirms first; commit or stash beforehand). <code>add --style</code> on a project with installed components keeps the recorded style and prints that apply instead, so new components arrive in the style the installed ones wear.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Fonts, chart, radius</BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>blaizio apply &lt;code&gt; --only fonts</code> / <code>--only tokens</code>, or install a font item directly (<code>add font-inter</code>).</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Icon set</BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>blaizio apply --icons &lt;set&gt;</code> retargets <code>BzGlyphs.cs</code> and installs the set's package. Use <code>--icons tabler</code> to go back to the default. See <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="docs/cli#icon-sets">Icon sets</a>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">RTL / pointer</BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>blaizio add --rtl</code> / <code>blaizio add --pointer</code>. A wiring-only run is a complete operation. To turn one off again, edit <code>blaizio.json</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Thin scrollbars</BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>blaizio add --scrollbar</code> appends the <code>scrollbar-thin</code> block to the tokens file. To turn it off, delete that block; to restyle the bar, edit it.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Tailwind input (<code>css</code>)</BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>blaizio add --css &lt;path&gt;</code> records the file and syncs the managed imports into it, plus the token block when it is missing.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Tailwind pipeline</BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>blaizio tailwind setup --mode &lt;id&gt;</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Registry</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Edit <code>registry</code> in <code>blaizio.json</code> (or <code>registry add</code> for named extras); <code>--registry</code> overrides per run.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Namespace, output dir</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>No dedicated command: edit <code>namespace</code> / <code>output</code> in <code>blaizio.json</code>, run <code>blaizio update</code> to re-install into the new shape, then delete the old directory and update your own <code>@@using</code> directives. (<code>-ns</code> / <code>-o</code> on <code>add</code> apply to that run only.)</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio registry</h2>
        <p>A registry is a folder of JSON served over HTTP or sitting on disk: an <code>index.json</code> catalog plus one <code>&lt;name&gt;.json</code> per item, with its file contents inlined. <code>add</code> accepts bare names (resolved against the registry base), full URLs or local paths, so private and third-party registries work with nothing more than <code>--registry</code> or a URL. Each item carries a <code>type</code>:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><code>registry:ui</code>: components. A registry built by <code>build</code> ships them as per-style <em>inlined</em> variants under <code>r/{style}/</code> (listed in the index's <code>styles</code>), and <code>add</code> resolves plain names against the recorded style's variant automatically.</li>
            <li><code>registry:lib</code>: shared helpers such as <code>utils</code>.</li>
            <li><code>registry:font</code>: a webfont selection (<code>font-inter</code> for the body, <code>font-heading-space-grotesk</code> for headings) that installs by patching the tokens file and the host page link instead of copying files. These items are generated from the same catalog the <a href="themes">Themes</a> controls use.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>This docs site serves its own build at <code>/r</code>. The <code>registry</code> subcommands manage which registries a project knows:</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Registry" />
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Subcommand</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Purpose</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>registry add &lt;@@NS=URL...&gt;</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Record named registries in <code>blaizio.json</code> (e.g. <code>@@acme=https://acme.dev/r</code>); install from them with <code>add @@acme/button</code>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>registry validate [PATH]</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Validate a source <code>registry.json</code> manifest (default: <code>./registry.json</code>).</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio generate</h2>
        <p>For registry publishers, <code>generate</code> scans a component source tree (the folder holding <code>Components/</code>, by default the current directory) and writes a <code>registry.json</code> manifest. It creates one item per component folder, infers dependencies between your own components from the source, and names the official components you build on, which you add as <code>@("@default/<item>")</code>. <code>--fonts</code> appends the catalog's font items, which only the official registry ships.</p>
        <p><code>build</code> compiles the manifest, and <code>registry validate</code> checks one without building. <a href="docs/registry/author-a-component">Author a component</a> walks through the whole flow.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Generate" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio build</h2>
        <p>For registry publishers, <code>build</code> compiles a <code>registry.json</code> manifest into servable output: per-item JSON with file contents inlined, per-style variants under <code>r/{style}/</code>, and the <code>index.json</code> catalog. Point any static host, or a plain folder, at the result and it works as a registry.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Build" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">blaizio mcp</h2>
        <p>Runs a <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Model Context Protocol</a> server over stdio, so an AI agent can use the registry on your behalf: search items, read a component's sources and parameter API, install components (with their dependencies), and check the project's configuration. The tools answer with the same JSON documents that the CLI's <code>--json</code> mode prints, so the CLI and the server share one contract.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Cli/Mcp" />
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader><BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50"><BzTableHead>Tool</BzTableHead><BzTableHead>Purpose</BzTableHead></BzTableRow></BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>search_items</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Search a registry for installable items, with paging. Results match the <code>search --json</code> shape.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>get_item</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>One item with full file contents and dependencies: what <code>add</code> would write, read-only.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>get_docs</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Docs URL, dependencies and the parameter API reference, matching the <code>docs --json</code> payload.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>add_items</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Install items and their dependencies as source. Files you edited are always kept, and installs from unrecorded hosts are refused, because the trust prompt cannot be answered here.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>project_info</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>The discovered project and its <code>blaizio.json</code> configuration.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
        <p>Installing needs an initialized project: run <code>blaizio add</code> once in a terminal first, so the wiring decisions (packages, Tailwind, configuration) stay yours.</p>
    </section>
</div>
